1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Write concern and read concern. What must be acknowledged, and which state can a read return?

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:10,000
Three voting, data-bearing members. C is cut off from A and B.

3
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:14,000
The stable _id names this one submission attempt. Keep an eye on it.

4
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:16,900
A applies the insert and records it in its oplog.

5
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:20,400
B has made the oplog entry durable, but has not yet applied it to its collection.

6
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:23,700
MongoDB 8.0+, default journaling: majority waits for durable oplog entries, not for applying.

7
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:27,200
So the client can hear "success" while B's collection still looks old.

8
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:32,800
Later B applies it. C never received it: it stays absent the whole time.

9
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:36,800
w: 1 asks only the primary. If A fails right after, that write may exist nowhere else.

10
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:40,800
Majority here is two. Count your own voting members; don't reuse "2" blindly.

11
00:00:41,300 --> 00:00:43,800
w: 3 needs C, which is unreachable. A stronger-sounding setting cannot finish.

12
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:51,800
Suppose the client asked for w: 3. A and B hold the write, C cannot answer.

13
00:00:52,300 --> 00:00:56,300
The wait stopped. The write was not undone: A and B still have it.

14
00:00:56,800 --> 00:00:59,800
Read back by the stable _id, and check the content matches the request.

15
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:06,100
A retry with the same _id cannot create a second copy. A fresh _id could: two tickets for one resident.

16
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:09,400
Read preference is routing. Sent to the primary, this read finds the ticket.

17
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:12,700
If a read lands on C, it finds nothing. That shows the state C saw, not that the write failed.

18
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:15,000
Read concern is about state: "majority" returns majority-committed data...

19
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:18,300
...but C's older majority-committed data can still predate 1099. Majority is not "latest everywhere".

20
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:22,600
During this partition, a read on C can return older data or wait/fail. That is the CAP choice.

21
00:01:23,100 --> 00:01:26,100
For a confirmation page, tie the read to the write: one documented recipe (timeouts still possible).

22
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:38,700
Three separate questions: Write concern: what must acknowledge before my write returns? Read preference: which member may answer my read? Read concern: which state may that read return? A timeout means "unknown": read back by a stable _id. A miss on a lagging or isolated member is not proof of failure.

